window.WM_STORIES = {"kroenten":[{"k":"t","html":"<p><strong>Distance: </strong>10.6km | <strong>Elevation Gain/Loss: </strong>956m | <strong>Total Time: </strong>3:46</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>It was the last chance to get up to the huts before the snow started to cover the mountains and make access hard until it was ski touring time. We were able to get a group of 5 friends together to go and tackle one last hut for the season.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The forecast was iffy and in the week prior to the hike, snow forecasts were showing a possible snow line of ~2,300m so we chose a lower elevation hut in Uri.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The Kroentenhutte sits deep within the Erstfeld Valley. You have to get a driving permit to even access the mountain road. Uri has many of these little hidden valleys you’ve never heard of and many of which haven’t changed for probably centuries.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>We parked at Bodenberg and set off for the ascent up to the hut.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_0590-edit.webp","w":787,"h":1400,"f":0.0103},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The valley gets almost no sunlight in the later months as the sun moves further to the south and the east-west orientation of the valley blocks out any sunlight getting to the valley floor. So, it was a very cold start with the overnight frost still coating every blade of grass and leaf.</p>","f":0.0207},{"k":"t","html":"<p>We made our way up the south wall and had to sneak between the Sulzplatten rockface which towers above you as you go up the dozens of switchbacks. As you emerge from the treeline around ~1,400 m ASL, the views open up greatly, and looking back to the east, you are rewarded with a clear view of the Sunnigen Stock ridgeline. In late October, the Autumn colors were on their last breaths but still painted the mountainside in deep brown and orange hues.</p>","f":0.2016},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_0594-edit.webp","w":1400,"h":787,"f":0.215},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Between Hutzi and Unter Geissfad, at around 1,460m there was a small mountain stream that we had to cross. Due to the lack of sunlight and cold morning temps, every rock and surface was coated in extremely slick ice which provided a fun challenge for the group.</p>","f":0.2284},{"k":"t","html":"<p>As you come around the curve on Ober Geissfad, the views to the east come into view and immediately hit you in the face. Oberseemanndli sits shaded in the foreground with the 3,100m Schlossberg wall covering the entire background and the glacier pours out of the alpine basin.</p>","f":0.3388},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_0600-edit.webp","w":1260,"h":708,"f":0.376},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The remainder of the hike is a quick walk up to the hut which is perched on a small rocky outcropping just north of Obersee.</p>","f":0.4132},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Built in 1890, the hut belongs to the Gotthard section of the SAC and is divded into two section: one being the original granite stone structure and a newer wood-sided area that houses the new dining and kitchen areas.</p>","f":0.4132},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The hut’s public spaces are all accessible even in the unstaffed season which is really lovely. It is also powered year-round by solar and hydro power, so it has all the luxuries of life - power, electricity, heat, etc.</p>","f":0.4132},{"k":"t","html":"<p>We arrived at around 1pm and scarfed down a lunch before dropping off our bags and heading up to the Oberseemanndli summit to get a better view of the glaciers and surrounding area.</p>","f":0.4132},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_0605.webp","w":638,"h":1134,"f":0.4132},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The walk was easy without our packs. There were a couple patches of snow to deal with from the snow storm the week prior but nothing that slowed us down. Once you are up on the ridgeline to the southwest of the summit itself, the walking becomes a bit precarious. The drop off is about 600m and is straight down. My camera couldn’t capture the perspective well but it is some white knuckle walking if you don’t like heights or experience any kind of vertigo.</p>","f":0.4132},{"k":"t","html":"<p>From the top, the views to the east are jaw-dropping. A low cloud layer moved into the Reuss Valley which created an awesome inversion effect with the peaks towering up above the sea of clouds.</p>","f":0.4132},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_0613.webp","w":919,"h":1400,"f":0.4132},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_0616.webp","w":787,"h":1400,"f":0.4132},{"k":"t","html":"<p>As the sun fell below the western peaks, we made our way back down to the hut as temps were going to drop quickly without any sunlight heating up the air. As we descended, the last bits of golden hour light painted the Schlossberg wall in a fiery glow with the hut sitting in the dark, shadey area in the foreground.</p>","f":0.4132},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_0631.webp","w":1400,"h":1033,"f":0.4132},{"k":"t","html":"<p>A few other groups showed up last minute and we settled into the evening of fondue and card games before all retiring for some rest.</p>","f":0.4132},{"k":"t","html":"<p>I woke up at 6am to let Leo out to use the bathroom and the conditions had changed dramatically.</p>","f":0.4132},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_0639.webp","w":787,"h":1400,"f":0.4132},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The group started to wake up and we were on the trail by 9am. The clouds began to recede from the peaks a bit which created a beautiful effect and appropriate “spooky” feeling given it was Halloween…</p>","f":0.4132},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc02236.webp","w":1600,"h":1200,"f":0.4132},{"k":"t","html":"<p>For the descent, we chose a different route through the Fulensee valley. We were hoping to get some good views of the glacier walls but the clouds had other ideas and by the time we got down to the valley floor, visibility had dropped to &lt;100m so we kept moving.</p>","f":0.5892},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_0663-edit.webp","w":787,"h":1400,"f":0.6773},{"k":"t","html":"<p>For the remainder of the hike, we followed the Alpbach river through a tight canyon, crossing it a handful of times. Much of the trail was covered in extremely slick fallen leaves which, in some cases, were just as slippery as the ice we experienced on the way up the day before.</p>","f":0.7946},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Just before exiting the river canyon, there was a beautiful waterfall tucked into the trees. It looked like something out of a storybook:</p>","f":0.912},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_0675-edit.webp","w":517,"h":919,"f":0.9413},{"k":"t","html":"<p>We wrapped up the last kilometer through the farm fields and were back at the car by 11am to head back to Zurich.</p>","f":0.9707},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_0677-edit.webp","w":787,"h":1400,"f":1.0}],"damma":[{"k":"t","html":"<p><strong>Distance: </strong>13.1m | <strong>Elevation Gain/Loss: </strong>913m | <strong>Total Time: </strong>3:18</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Some friends and I wanted to do a trail run to get our old, geriatric bodies moving in the mountains. Given we were going to run (speed walk really) we thought we could knock out a couple huts at once that were close enough.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The original mission was to do the Dammahutte and Chelenalphutte, which are both within the Goschenen valley above the reservoir. There is also the Bergseehutte but doing all three seemed way too hard. Well, even doing two was too difficult and we only had time to do Dammahutte.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_5839.webp","w":1400,"h":787,"f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The route starts from the top of the dam’s wall. The Göscheneralpsee dam provides 165 megawatts of energy via 4 turbines. Unlike most hydro dams in the country, this 155 meter tall wall is made entirely out of earth and the surrounding rock that was blasted during construction. It is also one of the main power production plants for the Swiss Federal Railways.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The initial part of the route follows the southern slope above the resevoir which carries you 200 meters up in elevation as you make your way to valley that sits below the hut and Damma glacier. It is a gorgeous basin with the bright blue glacial run off rushing its way down into the resevoir.</p>","f":0.1562},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_5844.webp","w":777,"h":919,"f":0.2142},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The hard part of the route begins here. You have to gain 500m of elevation in under a kilometer, implying an average grade of ~22%. We did the ascent in ~38 minutes according to my Garmin which is pretty respectable, especially for an old out of shape guy such as myself.</p>","f":0.232},{"k":"t","html":"<p>As I have said many times on this blog, I can tell how determined (or shitty) I am feeling by the gap of pictures in my camera roll. I have a photo of the beginning of the climb (the picture above) and then nothing until the hut where I have a picture of the cake I ate.</p>","f":0.3569},{"k":"t","html":"<p>While we were enjoying our cake reward, a helicopter flew right over the hut. It was not a REGA heli, so I assume it was resupplying some of the huts in the surrounding area.</p>","f":0.4462},{"k":"video","src":"video/damma-helicopter.mp4","poster":"video/damma-helicopter.webp","ar":0.563,"f":0.4462},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The hut is newly renovated and was booked out for almost every Saturday night during the summer months. It has a wonderful view of the Damma glacier to the west. The last remnants of the glacier are clinging onto the ridgeline.</p>","f":0.4462},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_5855.webp","w":1400,"h":787,"f":0.4462},{"k":"t","html":"<p>We started the knee-breaking descent back down to the valley. What took 38 minutes coming up, took 25 minutes going down. We realized we were not going to have time (or energy) to make it to the other hut and agreed that we would instead loop back to the starting point, up and around the northern part of the reservoir.</p>","f":0.5569},{"k":"t","html":"<p>We will need to come back next summer and do a running loop of the Chelenalphutte and Bergseehutte when we have more time and are in better shape as it would be a great loop and “two for one” to knock out.</p>","f":0.6123},{"k":"t","html":"<p>We kept a good pace and made our way around the bright blue, almost gatorade colored, lake.</p>","f":0.8339},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_5866.webp","w":1400,"h":787,"f":0.8615},{"k":"t","html":"<p>My two friends slept on the way back to Zurich.</p>","f":1.0},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_5838.webp","w":1400,"h":787,"f":1.0}],"albertheim":[{"k":"t","html":"<p><strong>Distance: </strong>6.2km | <strong>Elevation Gain/Loss: </strong>284m | <strong>Total Time: </strong>2:32</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>My mother and father in law were coming to Switzerland so we planned a true Swiss weekend: one night at a 5 star, fancy hotel and one night at a mountain hut. The two extremes of Swiss hospitality.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>We chose Andermatt as the place we’d enjoy the 5 star spot so Albert Heim Hutte was a natural choice as it is just down the road above Realp. It is also an easy hut to do with &lt;300m of elevation gain when starting from the Tatsch parking area.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The drive up from Realp is a wonderful experience and you get to stop at the iconic James Bond curve from the Goldfinger movie which has a famous car chase across the Furka pass.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_5755.webp","w":909,"h":1134,"f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The hike starts in an alpine tundra with just low meadows but quickly graduates into true alpine with the grass dying out shortly after the trailhead.</p>","f":0.0347},{"k":"t","html":"<p>For the entirety of the walk, you follow the Alpetli plateau to your west which is a gorgeous washout basin from the Tiefen Glacier.</p>","f":0.1042},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_5756.webp","w":1021,"h":574,"f":0.139},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The Tiefen Glacier’s outflow stream meanders through this flatland and the autumn colors brought orange and brown hues to the grassland surrounding the stream.</p>","f":0.1737},{"k":"t","html":"<p>A recurring theme on this Wanderlust blog is the observation of how quickly and dramatically the glaciers are dying. This walk along the Tiefen Glacier’s graveyard was especially eye-opening.</p>","f":0.1853},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The glacier has lost nearly 2km of length since the late 1800s. The hut was built in 1918 and even then, the glacier nearly came down to the hut. The GLAMOS data below shows how much it has shrunk in the decades since. The hut can be seen on the far right of the photo and the red line is the glacier’s boundary from 1850.</p>","f":0.1969},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/screenshot-2026-11-08-103801.webp","w":827,"h":585,"f":0.2085},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The remainder of the hike up to the hut requires just one steeper section to get up onto the rocky outcropping that the hut is placed on top of. Similar to the Planurahutte that I did just a month earlier, this hut feels like a bird’s nest perched atop this tower of rock.</p>","f":0.2953},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_5779.webp","w":854,"h":1021,"f":0.3197},{"k":"t","html":"<p>When we arrived to the hut, we quickly found out that the other guests staying were 30 middle school aged kids… it is common for Swiss schools to do summer field trips up to the mountain huts which is a much cooler field trip experience than I ever got to do as a kid.</p>","f":0.3475},{"k":"t","html":"<p>We spent the evening at the hut playing games, checking out the glacier, and enjoying a lovely meal.</p>","f":0.3475},{"k":"t","html":"<p>After the sunset finished and the clear night sky came out, I tried my best to capture some night photos of the stars but my Sony camera is not quite good enough for these long exposure shots.</p>","f":0.3475},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc02155.webp","w":1600,"h":1069,"f":0.3475},{"k":"t","html":"<p>We had a leisurely morning and after breakfast began our quick hike back down to the car.</p>","f":0.478},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc02191_01.webp","w":1274,"h":1600,"f":0.7064}],"rugghubel":[{"k":"t","html":"<p><strong>Distance: </strong>10.6km | <strong>Elevation Gain/Loss: </strong>555m | <strong>Total Time: </strong>3:59</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>A friend was visiting from the USA and wanted a proper Swiss mountain experience so we decided to do a day hike down in Engelberg. There are plenty of objectives in the Engelberg region that I haven’t tackled yet but most of them deserve, or require really, an overnight expedition. One that is doable as a day hike is Rugghubelhutte, which sits up on a high pleatu northeast of Engelberg.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>800 meters of elevation gain can be saved by using the Brunni gondola and chairlift system. It will quickly whisk you all the way up to Brunnihutte (not technically a federal SAC hut so I am not including it) and from there you have a quick walk along the southern aspect of the Sattelistock ridge.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc02084.webp","w":1600,"h":1069,"f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The hike is easygoing and full of families going up to the hut for lunch. About halfway there, you come across a small shepard’s village, Planggenstafel, which is a collection of half a dozen barns and sheds.</p>","f":0.2513},{"k":"t","html":"<p>We were doing the hike in late August so the cows were still up in the alpine and provided a constant cacophony of bell ringing during the second part of the hike.</p>","f":0.3266},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc02088.webp","w":1069,"h":1600,"f":0.3618},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The hut itself was originally built in 1883 as part of a national exhibition in Zurich. The hut was taken apart in Zurich and then hauled up, piece by piece, to the mountain where it sits now. They did this with just people and mules in 1884.</p>","f":0.5025},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc02095.webp","w":1600,"h":1069,"f":0.5025},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The hut has a lovely outdoor seating area that provides a panoramic view of the entire Engelberg village and valley. We were blessed with wonderful weather providing plenty of sunlight but also a cool cloud system that hung around the summit of the Rigidalstock.</p>","f":0.5025},{"k":"t","html":"<p>After a rosti with ham and cheese, we enjoyed a coconut cake and started the hike back down to Engelberg.</p>","f":0.5523},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Good to have you in town, Mark :)</p>","f":0.9503},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_5654.webp","w":1400,"h":787,"f":1.0}],"glarus":[{"k":"t","html":"<p><strong>Distance: </strong>39.5km | <strong>Elevation Gain/Loss: </strong>3,085m | <strong>Total Time: </strong>12:19</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"h","html":"<h3>Day 1</h3>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>I believe that at least once a year, you should partake in some form of self-inflicted physical torture, I mean test. The Glarus Grand Tour (a name I completely made up) was this year’s edition of that test. Originally planned as a 4 hut loop, I ended up only being able to tackle 3 of the huts as I simply did not have the energy, or time, to get to the last one. But, I feel this is one of the best multi-hut routes one can do in Switzerland. It has all the things you want in a good route: varying scenery from forests to alpine meadows to high alpine environments, it has rivers, lakes, and waterfalls, it has wonderful flora and fauna wildlife, and it is far from being overrun by day trippers and tourists - outside of the huts themselves, I saw less than 10 people on the trails during my 3 days of hiking.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The tour starts and ends as a loop at Tierfed in southern Glarus just beyond the village of Linthal. It is a <u>VERY</u> popular place because of the gondola to the alpine lake of Limmerensee, a spot that has unfortunately become viral on Instagram and among influencers. When I arrived mid-morning on Saturday, the car park was overflowing, and I parked on the road hoping my car wouldn’t get towed or ticketed upon my return 3 days later - thankfully, my windshield note and the kind Glarnerland people didn’t care about my car being there.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>This part of Glarus is special to me as it is where my great-great-grandparents, Jacob and Verena Freitag, emigrated from in the 1800s. The Elm and Linthal valleys were home to their families since the 1400s and maybe even earlier, though that is as far back as the records that I could find go.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The mission was to tackle the Fridonlinshutte, Gruehorenhutte, Planurahutte, and Claridenhutte. Sadly, the last one will need to be visited at a later date as my body didn’t have the energy to get to it on the last day.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The first few kilometers of the hike are along the forested road heading up the narrow valley between the Gamsistock and Hauserhorn peaks. Their steep walls tower 1,500m above you and provide much-welcomed shade throughout most of the day.</p>","f":0.0155},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The headwaters of the Linth River, which you are following up the valley, have carved many slot canyons. The rock has curves in it that remind me of the Antelope slot canyons in southern Utah - the difference being that the Utah canyons are soft sandstone and these Linth rocks are 150 million year old malm limestone that has been polished by the millions of years of water erosion.</p>","f":0.0458},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_5278.webp","w":827,"h":827,"f":0.0458},{"k":"t","html":"<p>As the tree line thins out around 1,200m asl, the path ahead presents itself with walls jutting up to the south and the massive Todi massif coming into view with its eastern glacier field pouring out of its summit block.</p>","f":0.1229},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc02003.webp","w":1600,"h":1069,"f":0.1229},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The Todi remains in view for the entirety of the tour and is always looking down on you like the Eye of Sauron.</p>","f":0.1229},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The route flattens out for only a brief moment along the Hinter Sand valley floor, where there is a man-made reservoir and grazing land for the alpine cows.</p>","f":0.1492},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The torture begins as you begin the 800m climb up the southern valley towards Fridolinshutte. The summer sun was reaching its apex in the sky as I started the climb. The forested spots were few and far between and completely disappeared around 1,500m asl so the full force of the sun and 30C heat was felt.</p>","f":0.1853},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_5284.webp","w":1400,"h":788,"f":0.2247},{"k":"t","html":"<p>One barometer of how much I’m suffering is the frequency of photos in my camera roll. This 800m ascent took ~2 hours according to my Garmin watch and I only have a total of 5 photos from the climb. While I’m sure the views were impressive, I just zoned out and put one foot in front of the other to get to the top.</p>","f":0.2602},{"k":"t","html":"<p>As I came over the last crest, the Fridolinshütte appeared like an oasis in a desert. I fell into one of the inviting lounge chairs and ate an apricot cake in about 3 bites.</p>","f":0.2808},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc02017.webp","w":1600,"h":1069,"f":0.2808},{"k":"t","html":"<p>I finally pulled my head up and looked around, appreciating the epic scenery that surrounded the hut on all sides. Most notable was the northwest wall of the Hinter Schiben mountain ridge, with an odd orange stripe running along its entire length.</p>","f":0.2808},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc02010.webp","w":1600,"h":1069,"f":0.2808},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Some desktop research tells me the line is known as the Röti (red) Dolomite, formed in the late Triassic period 210 million years ago, and has a high iron content, which gives it a reddish color. The same band runs along the Todi massif to the south of the hut as well:</p>","f":0.2808},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc02033.webp","w":1600,"h":1069,"f":0.2808},{"k":"t","html":"<p>After some rest, I settled into the hut a bit and changed for dinner. Most of the other guests at the hut were there to climb Todi and had 3:00am alarms to get up to the summit before it got too warm the next day and the glacier began to move around in the midday heat.</p>","f":0.2808},{"k":"t","html":"<p>I had a swim in the small lake next to the hut and enjoyed a spaghetti dinner. There is a summer herd of sheep that wander around the hut and the hut team have built a fence to wall off these unabashed intruders from coming in and causing mayhem. Their bells create a constant, but stereotypical Swiss, cacophony of ringing all evening before they settle down for the night.</p>","f":0.2808},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc02013.webp","w":1069,"h":1600,"f":0.2808},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_5317.webp","w":1400,"h":788,"f":0.2808},{"k":"h","html":"<h3>Day 2</h3>","f":0.2808},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Breakfast was some muesli, bread, jam, and alpine butter. As most guests left before sunrise, the dining room was much quieter for us normal folk eating at 7:00 am.</p>","f":0.2808},{"k":"t","html":"<p>I packed up my things, settled the bill, and headed south toward the Todi glacier field to go see the oldest SAC hut, the Gruehorenhutte.</p>","f":0.2808},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc02012.webp","w":1069,"h":1600,"f":0.2947},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The Gruehorenhutte was built 190 years ago as the first official SAC hut and served as a refuge for people wanting to summit Todi. It is really no more than a rock shack, rather than a proper hut, but was one of the first buildings in the Alps to provide a roof and protection from the elements. So, the Todi became known as a “first class” destination since it had such comfortable accommodation at its base - how times have changed!</p>","f":0.2993},{"k":"t","html":"<p>I dropped my backpack behind a boulder so I could run up to the hut and save some time for the long day ahead. The trail abruptly changes from grassy meadows to pure glacial rock fields at 2,150m asl.</p>","f":0.298},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_5323.webp","w":709,"h":1260,"f":0.298},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The outwash plain of the Biferten glacier sits below to the east and is a sad reminder of how quickly the glaciers are receding. The last 100m up to the hut are covered with chains to assist with the steep sections. The little building is perched on a thin ridge line that couldn’t fit anything larger than the current structure.</p>","f":0.322},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_5332.webp","w":788,"h":1400,"f":0.322},{"k":"t","html":"<p>I read some of the information plaques in the hut which explained the history of the hut and the first ascents of Todi. The view from the hut is pretty epic with the glacier cascading down to the south and the Linthal valley to the north.</p>","f":0.3269},{"k":"video","src":"video/glarus-gruenhorn.mp4","poster":"video/glarus-gruenhorn.webp","ar":0.562,"f":0.3269},{"k":"t","html":"<p>I headed back down to Fridolinshütte to grab my bag and head out for the day.</p>","f":0.3772},{"k":"t","html":"<p>I first had to get over the Ochsenstock ridge which was an easy, relaxed ascent with a nicely graded trail. I took a brief break at the top of the ridge to test out my drone flying skills and attempt to take a video showing the sheer scale of this place:</p>","f":0.4028},{"k":"video","src":"video/glarus-ochsenstock-drone.mp4","poster":"video/glarus-ochsenstock-drone.webp","ar":1.778,"f":0.4028},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The trail continued around the northern face of the Todi massif and mostly followed the 2,100 topo line which made for fast and pleasant walking. There is a very pretty snowmelt stream at point 2237 just below the Ochsenstock ridge that would be a nice spot to wild camp in the shoulder seasons.</p>","f":0.4381},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The terrain alternates between tallus / boulder fields and grassy meadows for most of the trail as you descend down into Rossmattli. When you reach the valley floor and look west, the next objective really hits you… looking up the trail towards Piz Cazarauls you quickly appreciate how much climbing you have ahead of you to get to Planurahutte. This picture doesn’t do a good job of capturing the perspective, but it looks like an endless climb, and I can tell you from the future that it really is endless!</p>","f":0.4879},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc02042.webp","w":1200,"h":1600,"f":0.4879},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The idyllic alpine grassland gives way to a harsh grey landscape of scree that would be best described as lunar in its overall feeling. It is desolate and lifeless except for a few tiny flowering plants and moss that somehow survive in this hellish place.</p>","f":0.5187},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_5356.webp","w":638,"h":1134,"f":0.5187},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The gravel path is dotted with the red and white trail markings every few meters which, in many areas, is the only way to tell where to go as the constantly shifting rock from previous hikers covers up any semblance of a trail there may have been. As the route steepens, the gravel takes on sand-like qualities and for anyone that has tried climbing sand dunes knows, each step feels like a futile effort as you slide back down to where your foot started just moments before.</p>","f":0.5341},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Around 2,500m asl, the gravel starts to give way to more solid rock and there are a handful of chained sections. I put the trekking poles away and had to climb up these few sections on all fours. In a few spots, I felt as though I should have had a harness and been clipped into something but that wasn’t an option.</p>","f":0.5487},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_5358.webp","w":709,"h":1260,"f":0.5487},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The red and white trail markings became even more frequent as the trail all but disspeared and the hiking turned into scrambling up, down, and around rock ridges. The rock was smooth, almost polished in some spots, from millions of years of glacial coverage.</p>","f":0.5667},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Finally, after hours of climbing, the Planurahutte poked out of the ridgeline above.</p>","f":0.5863},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_5366.webp","w":709,"h":1260,"f":0.5863},{"k":"t","html":"<p>I stumbled into the hut which was empty on the Sunday afternoon with all the previous night’s guests already well on their way back down to the valley. I grabbed a coconut cake as all my snacks were long gone and enjoyed it on the hut’s helipad.</p>","f":0.5907},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_5367.webp","w":788,"h":1400,"f":0.5907},{"k":"t","html":"<p>My body, and mind, for that matter, were completely beat after the 5-hour, 1,500m ascent. I retreated to my bed and passed out for a couple of hours while some afternoon showers rolled in.</p>","f":0.5907},{"k":"t","html":"<p>I woke up as a few more guests arrived and popped out onto the hut’s terrace to finally appreciate the surroundings a bit more. Planurahutte sits like a bird’s nest on top of this little ridgeline nestled between Hinter Spitzalpelistock (what a name) and Piz Cazarauls. To the east, the Todi summit block occupies most of the frame. To the east, the jaw-dropping Hufi glacier field runs for kilometers down towards the Maderaner valley.</p>","f":0.5907},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc02043.webp","w":1600,"h":1069,"f":0.5907},{"k":"t","html":"<p>While its size is huge in absolute terms, the glacier is one of the fastest-decaying ones in the Alps due to its relatively low elevation and exposure to heat and lower snowfall amounts. It has lost 1,600m of its surface length in the last 150 years.</p>","f":0.5907},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The other few guests and I enjoyed a delicious beef stew dinner and everyone headed out onto the terrace to enjoy the sunset over the glacier.</p>","f":0.5907},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc02047.webp","w":1600,"h":1069,"f":0.5907},{"k":"t","html":"<p>I got permission from the hut warden to fly the drone over the glacier and capture a view of the hut in the golden light. It was one of the most beautiful moments I’ve been lucky enough to enjoy in the mountains:</p>","f":0.5907},{"k":"video","src":"video/glarus-planura-drone.mp4","poster":"video/glarus-planura-drone.webp","ar":1.778,"f":0.5907},{"k":"t","html":"<p>After a few card games, I retired for the night with a beaten body but a fulfilled spirit, having made it to my highest hut yet and in a place that my family had called home for centuries.</p>","f":0.5907},{"k":"t","html":"<p>I woke up at 3:30am for a bathroom break and got a few photos of the starry night sky over the glacier, though my small but mighty Sony RX100 isn’t quite good enough to get crisp nighttime photos:</p>","f":0.5907},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc02053.webp","w":1600,"h":1069,"f":0.5907},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc02056.webp","w":1600,"h":1069,"f":0.5907},{"k":"h","html":"<h3>Day 3</h3>","f":0.5907},{"k":"t","html":"<p>I woke up around sunrise and went outside to see the sun come over the Todi to the east.</p>","f":0.5907},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc02061.webp","w":1600,"h":1069,"f":0.5907},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The most striking geological feature near the Planurahutte is the wind scoop glacier face below the Spitzalpelistock peak. It is 500m long wall that has been perfectly carved by the wind to create this sculpted facade of ice:</p>","f":0.5907},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc02048.webp","w":1069,"h":1600,"f":0.5907},{"k":"t","html":"<p>I scarfed down the breakfast of porridge and bread and then set off for the very, very long descent back down to Tierfed.</p>","f":0.5907},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The high alpine rock and gravel sections were much more enjoyable with fresh legs and a downhill direction. The other hut guests joined me for the hike down and we made great time getting down to Chuestafeli. The view over the Hinter Sand valley from this spot was gorgeous and reminded me of Yosemite valley for some reason:</p>","f":0.693},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc02073.webp","w":1600,"h":1069,"f":0.7135},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Dropping down into Hinter Sand was an absolute slog. According to the GPS file from my watch, there was a total of 63 switchbacks during the 700m descent from the upper valley. We all commented that towards the end we felt almost dizzy from the constant turning around - a merry go round from hell. In retrospect, I am glad I ascended through the other valley to Fridolinshutte the two days prior as this ascent path would have made me cry.</p>","f":0.8363},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Once we were down in Hinter Sand, the shadow of Selbsanft provided cooler temps for the remainder of the easy walk down to Tierfed.</p>","f":0.9386},{"k":"t","html":"<p>We got back to the car exactly 4 hours after we started that morning. With sore feet and happy smiles, we headed back to Zurich.</p>","f":1.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Thanks for the memories, Glarus. See you soon.</p>","f":1.0},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc02021.webp","w":1069,"h":1600,"f":1.0}],"glarnisch":[{"k":"t","html":"<p><strong>Distance: </strong>16.4km | <strong>Elevation Gain/Loss: </strong>1,151m | <strong>Total Time: </strong>3:45</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>After a quick “wake me up” cold shower and rosti breakfast, I was out the door and on the road by 6:30am. The original plan was to tackle a double hut route in Goschenen but the Gotthard Tunnel traffic added an extra hour to the drive. So instead, I called an audible and headed towards Glarus, where I still have many huts to tackle. I had to do a bit of on-the-road thinking and decided to head up to Klontal Lake and Glärnischhütte.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Originally built in 1868 as a small refuge shack, the Glärnischhütte today sits on the western slope of the Glärnisch, a massive mountain block in the center of its eponymous canton of Glarus. The Glärnisch measures over 10km east to west and nearly 7km south to north. The Glärnisch glacier which lays to the east of the hut is on its last legs. When the hut was originally built, the glacier came all the way down next to the hut, as shown in the image below from <a href=\"https://glamos.ch/en/mapviewer#/A50k-04\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GLAMOS, the Swiss glacier research group</a>. The red outline shows the glacier’s size back in 1875:</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/screenshot-2026-07-12-123724.webp","w":1021,"h":402,"f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The current structure of the Glärnischhütte was built in 1885 with recent 2023 renovations adding a modern structure and expanding the sleeping capacity to 90 people. Renovation periods usually force wardens to shut down for entire seasons, resulting in lost income for over a year in some cases. The Glärnischhütte decided to try something different in 2023 while the hut underwent work and set up a <a href=\"https://www.exped.swiss/de/ein-basecamp-entsteht\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Himalaya-style basecamp</a> next to the hut in the alpine zone.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/screenshot-2026-07-12-113643.webp","w":1397,"h":783,"f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>My approach started at the Klöntal parking lot, which was already full at 7:45am on Saturday morning; it seemed the crazy heat wave was driving everyone up to the mountains to find cooler air.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The first kilometers are on the gravel road leading up the Rossmatter valley. The first 2km averages a 21-degree incline. At 1,200m asl the trees begin to thin a bit and a beautiful valley floor presents itself with a meandering river and dramatic views to the south east with the Gassenstock wall rising above everything else.</p>","f":0.2352},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_4634.webp","w":850,"h":1134,"f":0.2352},{"k":"t","html":"<p>I took a brief break in Käsernalp, a tiny hamlet of a dozen or so houses that I assume are mostly for the alpine farmers and second summer homes for folks living in the surrounding area. There is a small guest house and a kiosk to purchase snacks and drinks. It feels like the Shire from The Lord of the Rings if LOTR were set in the Alps.</p>","f":0.2514},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_4637-edit.webp","w":766,"h":1021,"f":0.2514},{"k":"t","html":"<p>I continued up the mountain road which briefly flattened out in the Rossmatt bowl with the Firnbach waterfall cascading down the eastern wall.</p>","f":0.3117},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_4657-edit.webp","w":689,"h":919,"f":0.3117},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Now the hard work began. I had 2.2km and over 600m to get to the hut, implying a 31 degree average incline for the remainder of the climb. My .gpx route shows roughly 40 switchbacks.</p>","f":0.349},{"k":"t","html":"<p>I was very happy that I left as early as I did as the temperature was still not too hot. If I were attempting to do this in the early afternoon heat, I doubt I would have been able to finish. With each step, the views opened up and the clouds evaporated away leading to epic views of the surrounding peaks</p>","f":0.3897},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01847.webp","w":1600,"h":1069,"f":0.4164},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The climb was fairly monotonous with constant switchbacks. There was a short section that required holding onto chains to get up and over a small cliff section. The bugs, mostly horseflies, were overwhelming and if I stood still for even 30 seconds they’d swarm me… good motivation to keep moving.</p>","f":0.4466},{"k":"t","html":"<p>As I rounded the last cliff section, the hut came into view. It’s position is one of a bird’s nest, precariously placed on top of a rocky outcropping amid a large talus field.</p>","f":0.4737},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01849.webp","w":1600,"h":1027,"f":0.5028},{"k":"t","html":"<p>I was the first person to arrive that morning and the hut team was enjoying the morning air on the outdoor porch working on meal prep. A few ladies were pealing oranges and apricots for their cakes and fruit bowls to serve later that day and the air filled with sweet citrus, something your nose doesn’t expect to smell up at 2,000m in the mountains.</p>","f":0.5028},{"k":"t","html":"<p>I chugged a coke and scarfed down two cakes - one chocolate and one plum - and enjoyed the views. My legs and feet needed some rest after tackling the 7.5km + 1,100m gain climb in exact 2 hours. I purchase a souvenir from the hut shop, a custom hut branded headband for ski touring, and then started the run back down the valley.</p>","f":0.5028},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01846.webp","w":1600,"h":1069,"f":0.5028},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The run back down the mountain was fast. My watch shows an average pace of 7-9min per kilometer and a elevation drop pace of 1,500m per hour. Thankfully, the trail had just a few people coming up at that hour so trail traffic wasn’t too bad.</p>","f":0.652},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Once I reached the valley floor, my legs were reaching the end of their stamina and the remainder of the descent was 50/50 running vs. fast walking as my quads began to gave out. Half way down, I had a brief puking session from the extreme heat combined with my overexertion but I will save you the details.</p>","f":0.8011},{"k":"t","html":"<p>I made it back to the car, had a brief cold soak of my legs in the river, and headed back for Zurich.</p>","f":1.0}],"sewen":[{"k":"t","html":"<p><strong>Distance: </strong>7.4km | <strong>Elevation Gain/Loss: </strong>642m | <strong>Total Time: </strong>4:51</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Another weekend, another hut! We decided to head to Susten Pass with a few friends to tackle another hut. The dogs came with us as well this time to get some much-needed mountain time and exercise.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Built in 1970, the hut sits on a southern outcropping below the summit block of Ruederstock. It was destroyed just 3 years after construction by an avalanche and subsequently rebuilt in more protected terrain.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The most common approach is the family-friendly ascent starting from the Gitzichrummenflue parking lot on the way up to the Susten Pass just a few more kilometers to the west.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01824.webp","w":1600,"h":1069,"f":0.0169},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The approach is entirely uphill with almost no flat spots. It is a well-maintained trail with only a few spots that require hand balancing on some bigger steps, but it only took us 1.5 hours to get to the hut.</p>","f":0.1355},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The cascading waterfalls on the southern valley wall were beautiful as they flowed off the Rutifirn glacier below the Griessenhorn. The glacier is clearly on its last legs and likely won’t exist in the next few years at current melting rates.</p>","f":0.2032},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The hut design is quite similar to the Voralphutte, which is just south of the Griessenhorn, pictured above.</p>","f":0.3048},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01836.webp","w":1600,"h":1049,"f":0.3387},{"k":"t","html":"<p>We enjoyed a hearty lunch of Alpine macaroni, Spätzle, and apple most before starting our descent back down via the Seewenalp lake route to the east.</p>","f":0.3387},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01837.webp","w":1600,"h":1069,"f":0.3387},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The small lake is a wonderful pit stop and an inviting swim, something our dogs were happy to accept.</p>","f":0.504},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_4573.webp","w":945,"h":1260,"f":0.504},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The remainder of the hike follows this ridge spine to the south east and then drops down into the tree line. It is a much nicer descent than the way up given the lower slope angle. One of our pups got hurt on the way down so our progress was slow as we had to carry him for a bit and take it slow.</p>","f":0.6693},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01841.webp","w":1600,"h":1069,"f":0.7024},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Right before you drop into the tree line, the view to the west looking up to the Susten Pass is the most dramatic vista of the route. The Wendenhorn rises above the competing summits surrounding it and the green foreground provides gorgeous contrast to the starkly beautiful snowfields and black rock summit blocks.</p>","f":0.8016},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01845.webp","w":1600,"h":1069,"f":0.8016}],"salbit":[{"k":"t","html":"<p><strong>Distance: </strong>7.9km | <strong>Elevation Gain/Loss: </strong>909m | <strong>Total Time: </strong>5:33</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>This will be a short one. A few friends and I decided to do a short day hike up to Salbithutte in the same Goschenen Valley that I did <a href=\"#voralp\">the Voralphutte hike</a> a few weeks earlier.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Built in 1930, the primarily granite structure sits halfway up the northern valley wall. The famous Jakob Eschenmoser led the expansion of the hut in 1966.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The hut is a popular climbing basecamp given the plethora of large granite faces that create a bit of a cirque around the hut, including the dramatic Salbitschijen, which has knife-like obelisks jutting up from its summit. This is not my photo but it provides a great example of these extreme rock structures on the peak:</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_6770-800x600.webp","w":800,"h":600,"f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>We started the hike in the same Grit parking lot as the Voralphutte hike began at. I don’t think there is a single flat step taken along the entire route. It is nearly 1,000m of elevation gain in a short 3.5km approach, or an average grade of ~30%.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Much of the hike is in the pine forest, so views don’t really become available until 1,800m asl when the trees begin to dwindle away. Once we emerged from the forest, the 180-degree views opened up with the Goschenen Valley below, Dammastock to the west, and Brunnenstock to the east.</p>","f":0.2852},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01814.webp","w":1600,"h":1069,"f":0.2852},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Real-feel temperatures on the day we went were hovering around 37C (100F), with a scorching sun beating down even in the mid-morning. While the pine forest blocked the mountain views, it did provide lovely shade from the UV index of 7.</p>","f":0.3203},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The Salbitschijen came into view as it hovered over the surrounding peaks with its dramatic summit.</p>","f":0.4133},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01816.webp","w":1374,"h":1600,"f":0.4133},{"k":"t","html":"<p>A few more steps and we made it to the hut. It is nestled into a small hump on the high plateau, giving it a sort of military bunker feeling.</p>","f":0.5063},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01817.webp","w":1600,"h":1069,"f":0.5063},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The background was filled up by the snowfields and glacier below Dammastock, Tiefenstock, and Schneestock. The extreme temps made the air slightly hazy so the further views weren’t as clear as I’d hoped but it was still a gorgeous setting for our outdoor lunch.</p>","f":0.5063},{"k":"t","html":"<p>After our soup and yak sausage (a first for me), we began our knee-breaking descent back down to the car.</p>","f":0.5557},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01821.webp","w":1069,"h":1600,"f":0.7532}],"piansecco":[{"k":"t","html":"<p><strong>Distance: </strong>10.2km | <strong>Elevation Gain/Loss: </strong>375m | <strong>Total Time: </strong>4:15</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Our family was in town for the weekend and wanted to experience a SAC hut. We picked Capanna Piansecco near Airolo, as it is a family-friendly hike for our younger niece.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The hut is one of the lowest-elevation huts in the SAC portfolio, sitting at the tree line above the Bedretto Valley. The hut was originally a military barracks with minimal facilities. The entire Bedretto / Gotthard region was militarized for much of Switzerland’s history. Still today, there is a monstrous military barracks, the <a href=\"https://maps.app.goo.gl/wriG739NEGZHMyTN9\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Airolo Caserma Bedrina</a>, at the east end of the valley.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Along the trail there are a handful of old bunkers that’ve mostly succumbed to Mother Nature.</p>","f":0.05},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_4161.webp","w":850,"h":1134,"f":0.05},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The hike begins from a small parking lot managed by a goat farmer along the road up to the Nufenen Pass. It is 5 CHF per day and is entirely honor-system-based, a wonderful attribute of Swiss culture…</p>","f":0.05},{"k":"t","html":"<p>For much of the hike, there are massive electrical towers every few hundred meters. These carry power from various hydropower plants in the Gotthard / Bedretto region to the neighboring Valais and Ticino regions. The towers over our head were transmitting power from the <a href=\"https://s.geo.admin.ch/htev40eijk60\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Robiei Plant</a> and <a href=\"https://s.geo.admin.ch/zbizmsvpvwbd\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Lucendro Plant</a>.</p>","f":0.1501},{"k":"t","html":"<p>While they’re a bit of an eyesore compared to the surrounding mountain views, it’s a good reminder of the Swiss culture of preserving the old while creating some of the most impressive modern infrastructure in the world.</p>","f":0.1626},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_4150.webp","w":850,"h":1134,"f":0.1751},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The hike itself is very calm and easy to navigate. It follows the 2,100m contour line for much of the 5km hike to the hut. The lackluster 2025/2025 snow season was easily visible, with very little snow hanging onto the peaks on either side of the valley.</p>","f":0.2502},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01758.webp","w":1069,"h":1600,"f":0.2752},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The north wall of the Bedretto valley is well-known for climbing and the granite walls dominate your view for the entire hike. The Manio pass which splits the Poncione di Cassina Baggio and Poncione di Manio is especially striking and would be an incredible ski touring descent to come back to one day.</p>","f":0.3253},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01762.webp","w":1021,"h":766,"f":0.3503},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The hike descends into the tree line as it approaches the Piansecco hut and the small mountain streams provide lovely hydration stops every so often. The hut comes into view as you round the corner into the Alpe di Rotondo area, which funnels the snowmelt from the Chueboden Glacier up above. The cabin was renovated in 2020 and the bright orange Larch wood siding makes the cabin really stand out amid the surrounding greenery.</p>","f":0.4253},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01769.webp","w":1600,"h":1050,"f":0.4504},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The Rotondo area just before the hut is a great spot to spend time. The stream has some of the clearest water I’ve ever seen in my life, and the transition zone between the tree line and the alpine is always one of my favorite environments. Large boulders left behind by ancient glaciers that pushed them down the mountain dot the valley floor.</p>","f":0.4654},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01773.webp","w":1168,"h":1600,"f":0.4654},{"k":"t","html":"<p>We arrived at the hut in the late afternoon and got settled in before a nice family dinner. The hut is certainly the nicest one I’ve visited so far, with brand new bedrooms, bathrooms, and common areas. The area around the hut has areas for kids to play and plenty of outdoor seating to take in the views.</p>","f":0.5004},{"k":"t","html":"<p>We watched as the sun fell below the mountains and the peaks across the valley lit up in a fiery glow.</p>","f":0.5004},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01787.webp","w":1400,"h":878,"f":0.5004},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The next morning, we enjoyed the hearty lunch of yogurt, oats, cheeses, and fruit before heading back the way we came. Piansecco is a easy objective and should be at the top of the list for any families that want to dip their toes into the SAC experience!</p>","f":0.6503},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_4171.webp","w":850,"h":1134,"f":0.8501}],"legler":[{"k":"t","html":"<p><strong>Distance: </strong>12km | <strong>Elevation Gain/Loss: </strong>781m | <strong>Total Time: </strong>3:36</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>A friend from the States was passing through Switzerland for a day so I decided to show him a proper Swiss experience by heading up to a SAC cabin. He was coming from Liechtenstein and needed to head to Zurich after, so the Glarus region was the obvious choice. Leglerhutte quickly became the obvious choice given it was still early in the season and the higher cabins around Linthal / Todi were buried in snow.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Built in 1908, the Leglerhutte was named after the Legler family, a prominent textile family in Glarus who funded much of the first construction.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The hut sits within the Freiberg Karpf wildlife reserve, which is the oldest in Switzerland, at nearly 500 years old.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_3996-1.webp","w":766,"h":1021,"f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The actual hiking begins at the Mettmen gondola top station but it requires a bus ride from Schwanden first due to a recent rockslide that closed off the road to private vehicles. The Mettmen station sits next to the Garichti reservoir. There is almost no information on this reservoir anywhere online, and I saw little info on the trail, but I assume it was (maybe is?) a power source for the local communities. Regardless, it is a beautiful reservoir with the view of the Glarnisch wall occupying much of the frame when looking north.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01707.webp","w":1600,"h":867,"f":0.0408},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Much of the ascent is a gradual climb up through the Nideren valley floor. The early June timing meant the ground was covered in beautiful alpine wildflowers.</p>","f":0.1531},{"k":"t","html":"<p>About halfway up the valley the clouds began to cascade over the eastern ridge and filled up the valley bowl. It brought a sort of Scottish highlands or Icelandic vibe to the scene.</p>","f":0.2551},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01711.webp","w":1069,"h":1600,"f":0.2551},{"k":"t","html":"<p>As we began the final push up to the Sunnenberg South Pass, visibility went to basically zero as we entered the clouds. Snowline was around 2,210m ASL, but snow travel was easy even without microspikes.</p>","f":0.4504},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_4023.webp","w":1050,"h":1400,"f":0.4504},{"k":"t","html":"<p>As we rounded the corner to the hut, the clouds began to clear out, and the Glarnisch wall peeked out of the cloud layer.</p>","f":0.4847},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_4022.webp","w":1400,"h":1310,"f":0.4847},{"k":"t","html":"<p>We then arrived at the hut as the clouds lifted out of the valley. Our bribe to the weatherman must have worked. The hut sits on a flat platform at 2,273m and has sweeping views of the entire Linthal valley with Todi to the south, Glarnisch to the west, and the towns of Schwanden and Glarus to the north.</p>","f":0.5102},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_4019.webp","w":1260,"h":1129,"f":0.5102},{"k":"t","html":"<p>After checking in, we settled into the hut and met some of the other guests. After plenty of card games, a hearty dinner, and dessert, we packed it in for the night and got some rest.</p>","f":0.5102},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The sky cleared out around midnight and from my window I could see millions of stars but sadly my camera is not good enough to capture night skies…</p>","f":0.5102},{"k":"t","html":"<p>My alarm went off at 5:15am and I quietly rushed outside to witness one of the most beautiful sunrises in my life. Instead of trying to describe it, I will just share some of the photos:</p>","f":0.5102},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01721.webp","w":1600,"h":1005,"f":0.5102},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01720.webp","w":1600,"h":1069,"f":0.5102},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01719.webp","w":1600,"h":616,"f":0.5102},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01724_01.webp","w":1600,"h":809,"f":0.5102},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The purple haze painted the western faces of the surrounding peaks. It was completely silent with not even a breeze disturbing the calm.</p>","f":0.5102},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01727.webp","w":1600,"h":1004,"f":0.5102},{"k":"t","html":"<p>I spent at least an hour wandering around just in awe of the natural beauty. The mountains began to wake up, with marmots starting their morning calls and cow bells filling the valley with song.</p>","f":0.5102},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The hut awoke around 7 am and we all enjoyed a breakfast before packing up and heading out.</p>","f":0.5102},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The weather on the descent was completely clear, with white wispy clouds delicately hanging in the blue sky.</p>","f":0.5837},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01735.webp","w":1600,"h":912,"f":0.6082},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Given the area is an active wildlife reserve, I would have expected to see more alpine creatures but the only larger animal we saw was a young mountain goat perched up on a boulder quietly watching us go by. He was too far away to capture on camera and seemed to be alone which is rare.</p>","f":0.6816},{"k":"t","html":"<p>We made our way back down to the reservoir through the more elevated western route that hugs the Matzlenstock ridgeline.</p>","f":0.7796},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01732.webp","w":1600,"h":1069,"f":0.8041},{"k":"t","html":"<p>We dropped down to the gondola and headed back home. It was a lovely start to the summer hut season.</p>","f":1.0},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01733.webp","w":1600,"h":1069,"f":0.951}],"spitzmeilen":[{"k":"t","html":"<p><strong>Distance: </strong>11.7km | <strong>Elevation Gain/Loss: </strong>954m | <strong>Total Time: </strong>3:55</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>It was the Ascension day holiday here in Switzerland. So, I decided to <em>ascend</em> into the mountains like any well-respecting Swiss would.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The weather window was precarious to say the least with overnight storms bringing snow and winds in the early morning so my hut options were limited to those with fairly low elevation to avoid the worst of the snow and fairly short approaches so I could bail back to my car relatively easily. I also didn’t have hours to drive on Wednesday afternoon, so the list narrowed down fairly quickly, and I landed on Spitzmeilenhutte above the Flumserberg ski area.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>From Zurich, the Steinbrugg parking lot is a short ~65min drive. The drive up the Schistal valley is gorgeous and becomes quite very quickly. It was one of the few valleys in Switzerland I lost cell reception in, a rarity!</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The hut sits within the Sardona tectonic area, a <a href=\"https://unesco-sardona.ch/en/homepage\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">UNESCO heritage site</a>, and is a ski touring paradise in the winter.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_3482.webp","w":945,"h":1260,"f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>From the parking lot, it is a fairly easy 6km hike up 950m. Half of the route is on farm roads which provide a nice switchback ascent which gives a nice break to your legs between the more steep trail parts that essentially go straight up the slope.</p>","f":0.07},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_3486.webp","w":766,"h":1021,"f":0.0933},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The weather on the ascent was perfect with intermittent clouds and cool temps. Except for the handful of farmers doing spring prep work, I am certain I was the only person in this entire valley but there were plenty of animals along the hike. The Swiss Safari included a deer, a few rabbits, many marmots, a couple of ptarmigans, and a pheasant that defecated all over the trail as it flew away after I nearly walked on top of it accidentally while it hid near the trail…</p>","f":0.1634},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_3493-1.webp","w":1400,"h":679,"f":0.1867},{"k":"t","html":"<p>At 1,800m asl the snow started to show up. The area is filled with blood-colored red rock. The iron-rich \"sernifite”, as it is locally known, is 250 million years old and was part of the Permian desert before it collided with the European plate and got shoved up into the mountain structures it occupies today. In a way, I was walking on an prehistoric desert surrounded by snow, a bit odd.</p>","f":0.3497},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01641-1.webp","w":1050,"h":1400,"f":0.3497},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The last challenge was to get up and over the Sässli step, a 150m-tall cliff wall that the hut sits atop. There was a stubborn snowfield holding onto its last legs, occupying much of the trail, so I had to do some off-trail scrambling up.</p>","f":0.3827},{"k":"video","src":"video/spitzmeilen-drone.mp4","poster":"video/spitzmeilen-drone.webp","ar":1.778,"f":0.3827},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Once up on the Mad Plateau, the snow situation got a bit more serious. Thankfully, the constant wind and late season freezing / thawing created a thick crust which allowed for easy travel. The main trail section through the rocky cliff area though was complete covered in drifts so I went up and around for a safer route.</p>","f":0.4201},{"k":"t","html":"<p>After a few minutes, the hut came into view:</p>","f":0.4481},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_3503.webp","w":1050,"h":1400,"f":0.4481},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The clouds started to roll in as I got to the hut which was a bummer as I wanted to get photos of the Spitzmeilen peak. But, you will have to settle for a more professional photo of it as it quickly became shrouded in clouds:</p>","f":0.4667},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/20180115-wissmeilen-00101-2048p.webp","w":1400,"h":933,"f":0.4667},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Thankfully, the clouds broke briefly around sunset, so I was able to get a few nice shots of the Hochsfinsler and Steingassler peaks in their last moment of alpenglow:</p>","f":0.4667},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01645.webp","w":1600,"h":1069,"f":0.4667},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01647.webp","w":1600,"h":875,"f":0.4667},{"k":"t","html":"<p>After a meal and tea, it was time for some sleep…</p>","f":0.4667},{"k":"t","html":"<p>My 6:00 am alarm awoke me to an entirely different world outside:</p>","f":0.4667},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_3544.webp","w":1400,"h":959,"f":0.4667},{"k":"t","html":"<p>There was a brief weather window from 7:00 - 8:00 so I packed quickly, had my breakfast, put on all my winter gear, and went for it.</p>","f":0.4667},{"k":"t","html":"<p>On top of the plateau, winds were 30kmh gusting to 50kmh with snow coming down practically sideways. Luckily, only ~5cm of snow had actually accumulated so route-finding was not too hard and with boot spikes traction was no issue.</p>","f":0.5094},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Visibility was far better than forecasted and the faint sunlight coming through the cloud layer allowed kept some of the shadows so I could see hazards a bit better than I expected. The red rock covered in snow was beautiful amid the harshness of the blizzard.</p>","f":0.5094},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_3551.webp","w":1050,"h":1400,"f":0.5467},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Once I made it off the plateau, it became nearly dead calm, and the scenery, while different than the prior day, had a stark beauty to it:</p>","f":0.5467},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01653.webp","w":1600,"h":1069,"f":0.6267},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_3567.webp","w":1260,"h":1059,"f":0.6534},{"k":"t","html":"<p>As I made it through the freezing line, the snow turned into rain and mud. The abruptness of this transition zone always amazes me. Within 10min of walking you can go from a winter wonderland to a spring environment.</p>","f":0.7067},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_3575.webp","w":1260,"h":1181,"f":0.84},{"k":"t","html":"<p>A few more steps and I was back to my car. Thoroughly drenched, I quickly packed up and headed home:</p>","f":0.8667},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_3578.webp","w":1002,"h":1260,"f":1.0}],"voralp":[{"k":"t","html":"<p><strong>Distance: </strong>18.8km | <strong>Elevation Gain/Loss: </strong>928m | <strong>Total Time: </strong>6:57</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Originally built in 1891, the Voralp Hut belongs to my personal SAC Uto Chapter here in Zurich. It is part of the 5 hut circuit connecting the surrounding huts</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The rebuild of the hut in 1959 was done by <a href=\"https://www.sac-cas.ch/de/die-alpen/eschenmoser-und-neue-experimente-150-jahre-huettenbau-in-den-alpen-2-teil-18857/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Jakob Eschenmoser</a>, who is one of the most important figures in SAC history, as he was involved in most of the 20th-century new builds or renovations of SAC hut around the country.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The hike begins in the Goschenen Valley car park at 1,216m asl. The word “<em>goschenen” </em>traces its meaning from the Romansh word “<em>caschinga” </em>which means “<em>alpine huts”</em> so the valley’s name is the home of alpine huts, a fun coincidence.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>You follow the road up towards the Goschenen resevoir but turn to the north into the Voralp valley as the switchbacks begin. The Hohenberg and Gandscijen rock walls surround you as you begin gaining elevation.</p>","f":0.0644},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_3357.webp","w":766,"h":1021,"f":0.0644},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The Voralp river accompanies you the entire hike which provides a constant source of fresh water to keep hydrated. I was surprised the river wasn’t running harder given the 20C+ temperatures.</p>","f":0.1074},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_3247.webp","w":850,"h":1134,"f":0.1074},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Around the Mittwald point at 1,661asl, you can look up to the northeast and see the Salbitbrücke bridge, a 100m long suspension bridge that hangs 150m over a gorge. It was currently out of service due to rockfall damage.</p>","f":0.2457},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01559-1.webp","w":845,"h":1021,"f":0.2457},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Around 1,800m, just above the Horenfelli farmhouse area, the snow fields began. Travel was fairly easy with the snow being mostly stable and supportive of our weight without any snowshoes. Things got a big harder on slope sections but we only punched through a few times on thinner sections.</p>","f":0.3718},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_3260-1.webp","w":1260,"h":1246,"f":0.3718},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The views opened up as we came around the Flachensteinen (Flat stones) run-off area. The massive Sustenhorn (3,502m) imposed over the valley. Even this high up in the alpine area, it still had over 1,000m of prominence above the valley floor.</p>","f":0.4296},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/sustenhorn.webp","w":1400,"h":896,"f":0.4296},{"k":"t","html":"<p>We needed to cross the river to get to the hut but realized that all of the bridges had not yet been installed this early in the season. The river was also too deep or fast running in every area we checked and none of us were in the mood to get soaking wet or risk falling in the river.</p>","f":0.4833},{"k":"video","src":"video/voralp-river.mp4","poster":"video/voralp-river.webp","ar":0.562,"f":0.4833},{"k":"t","html":"<p>We moved higher up and found a snow bridge that looked and felt strong enough for us to cross safely. We crossed just a few hundred meters northwest of the Voralp Hut. As an award, we go a full view of the Wallenburfirn glacier at the end of the valley.</p>","f":0.5101},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01579.webp","w":1600,"h":808,"f":0.5101},{"k":"t","html":"<p>After a brief climb up the backside of the bluff the hut sits on, we made it to our home for the night.</p>","f":0.537},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dji_fly_20260502_62658-pm_0024_177774232.webp","w":1134,"h":851,"f":0.537},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The hut’s vantage point up on the rocky promontory makes it feel like you are in a castle with views looking all the way down the valley.</p>","f":0.537},{"k":"t","html":"<p>We unpacked, started the wood stove, and settled in for a lovely night in the mountains.</p>","f":0.537},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_3323.webp","w":850,"h":1134,"f":0.537}],"lidernen":[{"k":"t","html":"<p><strong>Distance: </strong>8.04km | <strong>Elevation Gain/Loss: </strong>705m | <strong>Total Time: </strong>3:35</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Built in 1944, the Lidernenhütte sits halfway up the southern wall of the Riememstaldner valley near Sisikon. It is a classic SAC hut with cedar siding and pine-paneled interior spaces.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>I visited the hut as part of a ski tour to the Chaiserstocksätz summit ridge southeast of the hut. The approach to Lidernenhütte is significantly shortened by utilizing the gondola and is easily accessible by families from the top gondola station.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_1026.webp","w":1400,"h":1214,"cap":"<p>Views from the gondola up to the start of the ski tour. Bluebird skies sit high above the valley fog layer.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The early-season snow pack, or lack thereof, made the approach challenging as we had to go around numerous small bluffs, exposed rocks, and plenty of ice from the thawing / freezing cycle.</p>","f":0.1584},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Massive 300m cliffs hover over you as you approach the summit ridge. On this day, there were a handful of climbers going for a summit on Chli Chaiser.</p>","f":0.3961},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_1035.webp","w":1320,"h":1400,"f":0.3961},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The summit ridge has a small spot to rest, eat, and transition skis but it quickly filled up with about a dozen people when we arrived. The views to the north looked over Lake Lucerne with Gitschen poking up in the foreground and Pilatus dominating the background.</p>","f":0.5282},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_1036-edit.webp","w":1400,"h":737,"cap":"<p>You can appreciate the bear that the approach proved to be with the various small bluffs and rock outcropping in the foreground</p>","f":0.5282},{"k":"t","html":"<p>To the southeast, the summit ridge drops off quickly into the Seenalperseeli alpine plateau.</p>","f":0.5282},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_1039.webp","w":1400,"h":788,"f":0.5282},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The descent along the eastern face of the Lidernen is nice with an intermediate slope angle. Some avalanche fields provide a bump here and there. The lower part of the descent was tricky to navigate as the rocky humps that created extra effort on the way up became a maze on the way down.</p>","f":0.7169},{"k":"t","html":"<p>As you come around the northern edge of Schmalstockli, the hut comes into view (see below image on the lower left) and a dramatic multi layered view of the alps to the west forces you to snap a photo.</p>","f":0.882},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_1045-edit.webp","w":1400,"h":737,"f":0.882},{"k":"t","html":"<p>We skied down to the hut through another maze of rocks and pine trees.</p>","f":0.9623},{"k":"t","html":"<p>After enjoying a well-deserved apple tart and rivella, we headed home.</p>","f":1.0},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_1048-edit.webp","w":1400,"h":1331,"f":1.0}],"loborn":[{"k":"t","html":"<p><strong>Distance: </strong>11.2km | <strong>Elevation Gain/Loss: </strong>706m | <strong>Hiking Time: </strong>5:07<strong> </strong></p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Let me begin by caveating that 1. the Lobornhutte is not an official SAC hut (rather it is an unaffiliated, private mountain hut of which there are 225 of in Switzerland outside of the official 153 SAC ones) and 2. I didn’t get close enough to the hut to really say I <em>visited </em>it. Regardless, this was a big overnight trip and was a unique route so I wanted to share it regardless in the spirit of providing beta to the internet (and to fill up the initial content on this site :) )</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>This route began at the top station of the Luftseilbahn gondola in the Lauterbrunnen valley.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Almost immediately, the big three famous Swiss mountains - Eiger (troll), Monch (monk), and Jungfrau (young maiden) - come into view and occupy most of the horizon.</p>","f":0.0877},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_4155.webp","w":1400,"h":1050,"cap":"<p>The Sphinx mountain station is visible at the bottom o the right saddle.</p>","f":0.0877},{"k":"t","html":"<p>We broke trail right before the Schwarze Schopf summit (pt 2285) and headed northwest towards Overs Sulsseeli where we spotted some fairly flat grassland to camp for the night. Fair warning for anyone doing this route, the water in Obers Sulsseeli becomes stagnant and gross in late summer / autumn so fill your water containers in the streams before then. This alpine area showed heavy cattle use so I imagine the stagnant water has a lot of bacteria and things you don’t want in your stomach.</p>","f":0.3216},{"k":"t","html":"<p>For those unaware, cows are much heavier than people and have hard hooves that are small to their relative weight. When you get hundreds of them roaming around a grassy alpine area, they make the ground a pitted mess that becomes one of the best places to sprain an ankle. Even with proper hiking boots, every few steps I felt my ankles and calves engaging so I wouldn’t completely tear every muscle and ligament in my ankle.</p>","f":0.4093},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_4191.webp","w":1400,"h":1175,"cap":"<p>The friendly (hopefully) trail traffic</p>","f":0.4093},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The views of the Big Three from our camp were hard to beat.</p>","f":0.4677},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01447.webp","w":1400,"h":741,"f":0.4677},{"k":"t","html":"<p>As the sun dropped below Nideri Sulegg ridge to the west, we scrambled up to the ridge trail connecting Hoji Sulegg and Chlys Lobhore. I hadn’t spent much time on true ridgeline trails such as the famous <a href=\"https://youtu.be/DOJkyFhhcC4?t=224\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hardergrat</a> ridge near Lake Brienz just to the north of where we were camped.</p>","f":0.5847},{"k":"t","html":"<p>When you are standing on a knife edge ridge like this, it is very easy for vertigo to set in. You only have 1-2 meters of flat(ish) area to walk and the nearly perfect straight line going in either direction of the trail messes up your vision, and therefore balance.</p>","f":0.5847},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01471-1.webp","w":1400,"h":893,"f":0.5847},{"k":"t","html":"<p>But the extraordinary views in every direction quickly made me forget the sketchiness of the ridge. The Big Three to our southeast were basking in the oranges and pinks of the setting sun. <br></p>","f":0.5847},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/dsc01480.webp","w":1400,"h":699,"f":0.5847},{"k":"t","html":"<p>And to the west the dying sun made its way down to the horizon with countless layers of mountain peaks</p>","f":0.5847}],"glattalp":[{"k":"t","html":"<p><strong>Distance: </strong>6.9km | <strong>Elevation Gain/Loss: </strong>125m | <strong>Total Time: </strong>1:30</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The Glattalphütte is likely one of the most easily accessible SAC huts in Switzerland. It sits atop a rock slab ridge roughly 200m from the top station of the Sahli-Glattalp gondola.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>My wife and I made a lovely day hike trip out of the SAC hut by going for a walk around Glattalp lake which is one of the more jaw-dropping large alpine lakes in central Switzerland. Flanked by 600m rock walls on each side, the scenery transports you to a place reminiscent of Patagonia or maybe even Gates of the Arctic Park in Alaska.</p>","f":0.15},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_3994.webp","w":1400,"h":643,"f":0.2},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The southern mountain ridge from Rot Nossen to Ortstock is especially striking with hundreds of small waterfalls carving cuts into the rock.</p>","f":0.35},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The picture below shows the rock layers and folds that are exposed. I am by no means a geologist but I assume this is rock that was folded onto itself as the mountains shot out of the ground however many millions / billions of years ago.</p>","f":0.45},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_4036-edit.webp","w":919,"h":616,"f":0.5},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Switzerland is notorious for its wonderfully clean water but the Glattalp Lake had some of the clearest water I have ever seen in my life. The bottom was easily visible even in the deeper sections.</p>","f":0.6},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_4003-1.webp","w":850,"h":1134,"cap":"<p>You can appreciate the bear that the approach proved to be with the various small bluffs and rock outcropping in the foreground</p>","f":0.65},{"k":"t","html":"<p>We ended the short hike with a brief lunch out on the patio of the Glattalphütte. It is a beautiful SAC hut with classic cedar siding and has some of the most awe inspiring views especially considering how easy it is to get to.</p>","f":1.0},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_4038-1.webp","w":1400,"h":1066,"f":1.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Hudson and Leo approved ✅</p>","f":1.0},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_3996.webp","w":1021,"h":766,"f":1.0}],"grialetsch":[{"k":"t","html":"<p><strong>Distance: </strong>15.7km | <strong>Elevation Gain/Loss: </strong>544m | <strong>Total Time: </strong>3:25</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The Chamanna da Grialetsch sits at 2542m ASL on top of the mountain pass connecting the Dürrboden valley to the west and the Val Susasca to the east.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>I used the Dürrboden valley approach, which is easily accessible by car from Davos.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>As you drive through the valley, you are transported to the Scottish Highlands with a wide valley floor, <em>boden, </em>populated by countless sheep, cattle, horses, and even yaks. Small family farms dot the landscape each with their own unique home that has likely been there for hundreds of years. It is certainly one of the more unique valley landscapes I’ve visited in Switzerland so far.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_3472.webp","w":1400,"h":1050,"cap":"<p>The Furggabach stream carves its way down the valley and eventually feeds into Davos Lake</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The hike is quite relaxed and gains 530m over 4.3km on very well maintained trail.</p>","f":0.0524},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The only real challenge, especially in the summer months, is the large population of grazing cows that are quite stubborn and care little about your desire to use the trail. While most of them are domesticated to the point of no real harm, it is always a bit nerve wracking to be around a mother cow and her calf - especially when you have two dogs with you as I usually do on these hikes.</p>","f":0.1047},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_3478.webp","w":919,"h":689,"cap":"<p>The friendly (hopefully) trail traffic</p>","f":0.1178},{"k":"t","html":"<p>As you reach the saddle between Raduner Rothorn and Chilbirtzenspitz, you are greeted by the alpine Furgga Lake. Cottongrass bordered much of the lake and was being whipped away by the wind being funneled through the pass.</p>","f":0.2226},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_3488.webp","w":1134,"h":986,"f":0.2226},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The hut sits on the up on a small bluff next to an unnamed pond and overlooks the massive glacial bowl formed by Piz Grialetsch, Piz Vadret, and Piz Sarsura, all of which have summits above 3,100m.</p>","f":0.2619},{"k":"t","html":"<p>As is the case in much of the world, the Grialetsch glacier is quickly dying as warmer weather and shrinking snowpacks erode it each year. From the hut, only a small sliver of the glacier is still visible. Decades ago, it must have engulfed most of the valley… what a sight that would have been to witness.</p>","f":0.2619},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_3489.webp","w":1260,"h":916,"f":0.2619},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Only after getting back to my car did I realize that I didn’t actually take a photo of the hut itself… I guess the mountain views took all of my attention away. So, instead, I will leave you with the Swiss flag photo I took instead:</p>","f":0.2619},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_3490-1.webp","w":1260,"h":1057,"f":0.2619}],"obererbs":[{"k":"t","html":"<p>While this is not technically an official SAC Hut (see more on the&nbsp;<a href=\"#\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">About</a>&nbsp;page for specifics on this point), I felt it was fair to include it as this was one of the earliest snowshoeing trips I did in Switzerland.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The Obererbs Ski Hut is set on the south-eastern face of the Gross Kärpf in the Canton of Glarus. This is a very easy day hike and is accessible even for beginners. Total hike time with a meal break at the hut was 2:41 with 396m of gain and loss. Full stats can be found <a href=\"https://connect.garmin.com/modern/activity/17765163820\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">here</a>.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The western part of the shown route is a fairly steep open face, and in the ~.5 meter snow we faced that day, it required some scrambling. For an easier route, I would follow the eastern leg of the route below which is mostly groomed winter hiking trails.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The weather on December 15th 2024 was complete whiteout conditions with &lt;25m visibility at times. The topographical map, GPX route, and select photos are shown below.</p>","f":0.043},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_9149.webp","w":1400,"h":1050,"cap":"<p>Visibility near the hut. My trusty Leo showing the way.</p>","f":0.3656},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_9146.webp","w":953,"h":1271,"cap":"<p>The western approach was very steep in certain areas and was used as a descending route for backcountry ski and snowboarders which could be dangerous at times in limited visibility.</p>","f":0.2366},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_9155.webp","w":953,"h":1271,"cap":"<p>The Skithutte provided a much welcome shelter from the blizzard.</p>","f":0.4301}],"hundstein":[{"k":"t","html":"<p>Our very first SAC hut visit was Hundsteinhütte in Canton Appenzell in the northeast corner of Switzerland.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The Appenzell Alps (I have not found a different name so that is what I call them) are a strange micro range measuring ~5x10km that is completely seperated from the main Alps range that spans across the central part of Switzerland. The small but mighty range is likely most famous for <a href=\"https://www.google.com/search?sca_esv=2c899562bebaec1d&amp;sxsrf=ANbL-n5xMdD11m7Tnk7zztwf5NK-p6DcZg:1767881464467&amp;udm=2&amp;fbs=ADc_l-aN0CWEZBOHjofHoaMMDiKpaEWjvZ2Py1XXV8d8KvlI3izczKHhF2wgz0AoHZ2f_yJ4zQ_V75tA8EUhOa7p9-hLUjRkQRw3ortlqM86wFGVmFRE4_aKLArAjS0MK6KBTAwKiMsdzPk2nvl2BRf6XxF19YZUxBbM2e2sXYkZTYqIk6PrWKK-HW6v4lJTbTUQIwLGWZla0JRIMEem1t2gOV2E1g9Iiw&amp;q=saxer+lucke&amp;sa=X&amp;ved=2ahUKEwi77MbVj_yRAxUk1AIHHY4vM4EQtKgLegQIDhAB&amp;biw=1707&amp;bih=847&amp;dpr=1.5&amp;aic=0#sv=CAMSVhoyKhBlLVJmME5ZMFZTZXF4MllNMg5SZjBOWTBWU2VxeDJZTToOeVQycmRPWUk5eWx5YU0gBCocCgZtb3NhaWMSEGUtUmYwTlkwVlNlcXgyWU0YADABGAcgvcbNsQgwAkoKCAIQAhgCIAIoAg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Saxer Lücke</a>, a dramatic pillar that resembles the peaks of Torres del Paine in Patagonia.</p>","f":0.0},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The hike begins from the Pfannenstiel parking lot and right away kicks off with an extremely steep gravel road. At times, it is hard to gain traction and it is unbelievable that cars can even get up this road but they in fact do.</p>","f":0.0402},{"k":"t","html":"<p>As you come out of the tree line near Samtisersee, the views come into view right away and they are simply breathtaking.</p>","f":0.226},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_7571-1.webp","w":1400,"h":1050,"f":0.226},{"k":"t","html":"<p>We timed the hike almost perfectly in mid-October. The fall foliage was painting the northern wall of the valley and the late autumn temperatures brought a dusting of snow already to the Widderalpstock summit in the background.</p>","f":0.2762},{"k":"t","html":"<p>This valley remains one of my favorite in all of Switzerland. Small shepards huts and farm homes dotted the valley floor and a babbling stream found its way down to the Samtisersee.</p>","f":0.3264},{"k":"t","html":"<p>There is a final steep 150m climb up to the Fahlensee alpine bowl where the Hundsteinhutte and guest house sit on the shores of this epic lake.</p>","f":0.4268},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_7604.webp","w":1260,"h":945,"f":0.5021},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_7599-1.webp","w":1021,"h":922,"f":0.5021},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The whole area resembles a scene out of Lord of the Rings with dramatic rock pillars jutting out of the earth.</p>","f":0.5021},{"k":"t","html":"<p>We enjoyed a lunch at the guesthouse near the hut and then made our way back down.</p>","f":0.5021},{"k":"t","html":"<p>The wonderfully cliche Swiss flag flying proud above us as we descended back to our car.</p>","f":0.7511},{"k":"img","src":"img/log/img_7634.webp","w":945,"h":1260,"f":0.8009}],"intro":[{"k":"t","html":"<p>The aim of these Logbooks is to document each trip to the 153 huts.</p>"},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Over time, I suspect the logbooks will evolve. For the easier approaches, the log might be fairly short and simple. For more involved trips, especially ones that are overnight or where I do multiple huts in one trip, I will try to be more detailed and provide information on the route, trip planning, equipment, gear reviews, etc.</p>"},{"k":"t","html":"<p>I will link my Garmin activity page (example <a href=\"https://connect.garmin.com/modern/activity/21247370783\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">here</a>), which will show complete stats for the hut approach - time, pace, heart rate, calories, and even sweat loss in case that disgusting statistic is of interest.</p>"},{"k":"t","html":"<p>Each logbook will also include an iframe of Caltopo and the .gpx route recorded by my Garmin watch showing the route, time, and other data collected along the way. Like this:</p>"},{"k":"t","html":"<p>I intended to use <a href=\"https://map.geo.admin.ch/#/map?lang=en&amp;center=2660000,1190000&amp;z=1&amp;topic=ech&amp;layers=ch.swisstopo.zeitreihen@year=1864,f;ch.bfs.gebaeude_wohnungs_register,f;ch.bav.haltestellen-oev,f;ch.swisstopo.swisstlm3d-wanderwege,f;ch.vbs.schiessanzeigen,f;ch.astra.wanderland-sperrungen_umleitungen,f&amp;bgLayer=ch.swisstopo.pixelkarte-farbe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SwissTopo</a> which is the main mapping tool used in Switzerland and is by far and away the single best map, route planning, and general outdoor tool I’ve ever come across in my life. But, unfortunately, SwissTopo was not cooperating when I tried to do embedded iframes on this blog while also hosting a .gpx file on the map so it would show my route. Secondly, Caltopo (what is shown above) does not allow for iframe’ing of the Swisstopo scanned map layer due to copyright issues I imagine. So, for now, it forces the iframe to use the open source maps provided by Caltopo. While they are still high quality, it felt appropriate to use the Swiss maps for a Swiss hiking blog. I will continue to work on this and maybe find a solution with more research.</p>"},{"k":"t","html":"<p>I will try to provide some route beta - unique information based on my experience - where appropriate. Given how well documented the Swiss alpine landscape is, especially by the SAC Route Portal, I don’t know how much new information I’ll supply. I am doubtful there is a single square meter of the Swiss Alps that haven’t been hiked over the last few hundred years given how old the trail system is and how small the country is.</p>"},{"k":"t","html":"<p>I will also periodically give some gear reviews especially if it is a longer overnight approach requiring a more serious gear load.</p>"}]};
